WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:04.340
- [Interviewer] All right, Chase.

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:05.660
Chase, where'd you grow up?

00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:06.853
Where are you from originally?

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.350
- Originally I was born
out in Hawaii, actually.

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:12.400
I moved out here probably
at about five years old.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:13.848
- [Interviewer] Oh really, which island?

00:00:13.848 --> 00:00:15.270
- Big Island.
- Oh really?

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:16.370
- [Chase] Kialua-Kona.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.860
Mom was from Alaska, you know,

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:22.349
got tired of the snow one
day, and just took off to.

00:00:22.349 --> 00:00:23.250
- (interviewer speaking softly)

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:25.052
- Opposites, you know.

00:00:25.052 --> 00:00:26.402
Met my dad out there, yeah.

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:30.520
So yeah, I grew up out
there for a little bit,

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:31.640
kinda remember some of it,

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:33.683
mainly like more pictures and whatnot.

00:00:35.104 --> 00:00:36.470
But, you know, they
were still pretty racist

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:37.639
towards white people a lot.

00:00:37.639 --> 00:00:38.838
- Oh absolutely, yeah.
- So my mom--

00:00:38.838 --> 00:00:40.110
- [Interviewer] It's like
being black in Mississippi.

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:41.050
- Yeah, yeah.

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:43.580
My mom had a lot of trouble
finding work out there.

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:44.413
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:00:44.413 --> 00:00:45.380
- So, I mean, after
about five years of that

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:47.552
she kind of got sick of it, and decided.

00:00:47.552 --> 00:00:48.385
(interviewer speaking softly)

00:00:48.385 --> 00:00:50.580
Yeah, moved to California. Oh,
so you've been out in Hawaii?

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:51.870
- [Interviewer] Yeah, (laughing)

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:53.332
I know what it's like to be
a black guy in the south.

00:00:53.332 --> 00:00:54.814
- Oh yeah, haoles, you know?

00:00:54.814 --> 00:00:55.990
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:57.520
They do not like that (mumbling).

00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.899
I mean, it's a little
bit more calmed down now,

00:00:59.899 --> 00:01:02.520
but back then they were
just, they were all over it.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.630
- [Interviewer] Yeah, it's very strange.

00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:06.590
- Yeah, so once we moved out here,

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:08.780
pretty much the whole
family, or immediate family,

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:10.780
picked up, moved out here to California.

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:15.117
You know, that's all here and there,

00:01:15.117 --> 00:01:18.910
but I grew up mainly out
in Santa Clarita, you know,

00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:20.810
kind of, I guess, the suburbs.

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:21.803
- Yeah.
- You know.

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:25.240
Spent most of my high
school career out there,

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:27.190
like you know, doing the whole football,

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:29.203
sports thing, you know.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:33.460
Had a lot of like rich
white friends, I guess,

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:35.640
you know which was kind of cool for a bit.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:37.440
But that's how I kinda got,

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:40.303
I guess, introduced into
a lot of drugs, you know.

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:42.970
The party scene, this and that,

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:45.400
kind of spending like every moment I got

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:47.770
trying to hang out and get high.

00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:48.633
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:54.860
- Basically after that,
lost the house out there,

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:57.260
I somehow ended up out
in Lancaster with my mom.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:04.410
That all ended up being kind of a burn.

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:05.293
I hated Lancaster.

00:02:05.293 --> 00:02:06.600
There's so many,

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:07.910
just like there's just
nothing out there really.

00:02:07.910 --> 00:02:09.217
- [Interviewer] Right,
you're right in the desert.

00:02:09.217 --> 00:02:10.810
- So, at that time I was already

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:13.260
kind of addicted to heroin.

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:14.213
- [Interviewer] Oh, you'd found heroin.

00:02:14.213 --> 00:02:15.780
- Yeah, I mean, I've been doing heroin

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:17.630
since I was 17 years old, you know.

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:18.465
I'm 30 now.

00:02:18.465 --> 00:02:20.100
- [Interviewer] Wow.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.050
- I'm actually not doing heroin anymore,

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:23.830
it's kind of advanced to fentanyl,

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:26.281
but we can get into
that a little bit later.

00:02:26.281 --> 00:02:27.930
- [Interviewer] Yeah.
(sirens wailing)

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:29.590
- So when I got out to Lancaster,

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:33.890
and my means of supporting
myself as much were gone,

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:36.833
cause I used to grow and
sell pot for a few years.

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:40.670
I didn't have the clientele
anymore, out in Lancaster,

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:43.670
so I had to figure something else out.

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:46.150
So I picked up and moved
myself down to The Valley,

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:46.983
with a friend of mine,

00:02:46.983 --> 00:02:48.350
and started basically selling heroin.

00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:49.240
- Ooh.
- You know,

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:52.070
jumping around Airbnbs, you know,

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:53.720
for about month or two at a time,

00:02:54.943 --> 00:02:55.930
you know, here, there, here, there,

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:57.980
and you're just like kinda selling drugs.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:00.963
Basically,

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:03.720
met up with a girl down there,

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:05.570
ended up getting into a relationship,

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:08.883
and basically by the time I was 24,

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:12.790
close to being 25, she was about 30,

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:14.470
and we got arrested.

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:16.480
You know, I had already had a couple

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:19.020
little run ins with the law.

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:22.390
So like all of it was
basically possessions,

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:24.160
and whatnot, so I had a
little rap sheet of like,

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:25.189
you know, a drug user.

00:03:25.189 --> 00:03:27.070
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:30.020
- So when I got arrested for,

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:32.110
basically she got arrested,

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:36.245
and got caught up by selling
to an undercover, you know,

00:03:36.245 --> 00:03:38.140
so it was all her doing, I was just there.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:38.973
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:03:40.026 --> 00:03:42.260
- And by basically meeting
up with our drug dealer,

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:45.380
and then her middlemaning a sack,

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:46.880
because there was three parties involved

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:50.282
they were allowed to charge
us with conspiracy of sales.

00:03:50.282 --> 00:03:51.115
- [Interviewer] Wow.

00:03:51.115 --> 00:03:53.430
- You know, luckily they only charged me

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:56.727
with about six months, I had
to do flat time, you know,

00:03:56.727 --> 00:03:58.730
and I had to six months in jail,

00:03:58.730 --> 00:03:59.563
it was probably the longest stint

00:03:59.563 --> 00:04:01.083
I've ever had to do in jail.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:04.510
Just a couple like
before that a month here,

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:06.500
maybe a couple of days, a week, you know,

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:08.210
but I'd always get out.

00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:09.660
But this time was six
months, and I was just

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:13.711
going to be that's it, six
months, no time off, nothing.

00:04:13.711 --> 00:04:14.853
And basically,

00:04:17.110 --> 00:04:17.943
was still with the girl.

00:04:17.943 --> 00:04:20.720
She only got off with
about a couple of days,

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:21.860
maybe a week, time served,

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:23.270
because it was the first
time she ever got caught

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:24.810
doing anything like that.

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:26.835
First time she ever got caught
doing anything, I guess.

00:04:26.835 --> 00:04:27.820
- [Interviewer] Wow.

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:30.033
- So the judge kind of
just let her off easy.

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:33.850
Me, I had the little rap
sheet I was telling you about,

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:35.517
so they were saying,
"Okay, well, you know,

00:04:35.517 --> 00:04:38.237
"you didn't really do
anything, but at the same time

00:04:38.237 --> 00:04:40.092
"you're looking more
guilty than you seem."

00:04:40.092 --> 00:04:41.879
- [Interviewer] Okay.
- So threw the book at me,

00:04:41.879 --> 00:04:43.750
and they gave me as
much time as they could,

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:44.860
which happened to be six months,

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:49.317
which wasn't too, too gnarly or anything.

00:04:49.317 --> 00:04:50.150
But, you know, once you get to about

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:51.120
like a couple of months in there,

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:52.764
you kind of get a little stir crazy.

00:04:52.764 --> 00:04:53.800
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:56.403
- But did that, and got out.

00:04:57.520 --> 00:04:59.840
So I decided to get back out
to Lancaster with my mom.

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:02.120
Figured, okay maybe I can do this,

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:04.700
and like I had already
gotten clean in jail,

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:06.037
pretty much, was just like,

00:05:06.037 --> 00:05:08.167
"Okay, I can handle the Lancaster thing,

00:05:08.167 --> 00:05:11.350
"nobody around, just whatever."

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:13.254
Well the girl was still around,

00:05:13.254 --> 00:05:14.680
so immediately after I got out,

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:16.080
I was back into drugs, you know.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:17.987
- Started using again.
- Yep.

00:05:17.987 --> 00:05:19.840
And a few months later, that's actually

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:22.653
when I got into my motorcycle accident,

00:05:24.365 --> 00:05:25.715
and basically lost my hand.

00:05:29.775 --> 00:05:31.725
I was in the hospital for about a week,

00:05:33.578 --> 00:05:35.470
because I was all on Medi-Cal,
so like they didn't really.

00:05:35.470 --> 00:05:36.584
- [Interviewer] Did you realize that

00:05:36.584 --> 00:05:38.401
you were going to lose
your hand right away?

00:05:38.401 --> 00:05:39.234
Or, how bad was the accident?

00:05:39.234 --> 00:05:40.873
- That actually is a funny,
like kind of little thing

00:05:40.873 --> 00:05:42.423
that I always think about, too.

00:05:43.304 --> 00:05:45.380
Cause basically when it
happened, I'd seen it all,

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:46.213
I was conscious.

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:50.040
So like my hand was pretty
much severed, you know,

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:52.310
barely hanging on by a ligament or two.

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:55.310
And I mean, I'm talking
about artery was cut,

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:57.274
like I'm bleeding in a pool of blood.

00:05:57.274 --> 00:05:59.256
- [Interviewer] What was connected?

00:05:59.256 --> 00:06:01.030
- Just maybe a flap of skin,

00:06:01.030 --> 00:06:02.220
and like the ligament that connects

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:04.700
my thumb and my next finger,

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:06.890
like I could barely do
that, and that only.

00:06:06.890 --> 00:06:08.136
- [Interviewer] And the rest of your hand

00:06:08.136 --> 00:06:08.969
was just hanging off?

00:06:08.969 --> 00:06:09.802
- Yeah, it was just hanging there.

00:06:09.802 --> 00:06:12.550
Just could not, I couldn't
feel it cause I was in shock.

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:15.320
You know, I just flew 30
yards in the like, you know,

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:16.573
in the air, and tumbled.

00:06:19.090 --> 00:06:21.290
Actually on my way to go pick up a sack,

00:06:21.290 --> 00:06:22.440
you know, funny enough.

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:28.437
And basically they took
me to the hospital,

00:06:28.437 --> 00:06:30.790
and they kind of shoved me
out as fast as they could,

00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:32.603
cause I was, you know, on Medi-Cal.

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:36.210
Shipped me off to San Bernardino,

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:38.520
which actually had a
physical therapy rehab

00:06:40.417 --> 00:06:42.180
that I stayed in about three weeks,

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:44.445
about four weeks, or something like that,

00:06:44.445 --> 00:06:46.630
cause I mean, I just
wanted to get out of there.

00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:48.563
Cause not only was the,

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:51.450
I wasn't really dope sick,

00:06:51.450 --> 00:06:53.270
you know, from not having any dope,

00:06:53.270 --> 00:06:54.730
cause they had me on Percocets.

00:06:54.730 --> 00:06:55.640
And while I was in the hospital

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:57.410
they had me on Dilaudid and stuff,

00:06:57.410 --> 00:06:59.400
but I had like the whole mental issue

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:00.233
of everything, you know.

00:07:00.233 --> 00:07:01.500
The depression was kicking in, you know.

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:02.384
- [Interviewer] Sure.

00:07:02.384 --> 00:07:03.585
- You know, and like

00:07:03.585 --> 00:07:05.272
all that restlessness,

00:07:05.272 --> 00:07:07.840
and I'm broken kneecap,
severed hand, like, you know,

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:08.800
I was pretty messed up,

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:09.660
so like I couldn't really walk around.

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:11.240
I was in a wheelchair,

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:13.160
and I had to have assistance
getting out of bed,

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:14.860
into a wheelchair, like, you know.

00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:18.300
So that restlessness was
killing me, you know,

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:20.270
I was just stuck in a bed, you know.

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:22.240
Nobody that I knew was around me,

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:25.370
so the depression just
got crazier, and crazier,

00:07:25.370 --> 00:07:27.270
and I was just like, "I
need to get out of here."

00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:29.110
So I mean, I pushed to, basically,

00:07:29.110 --> 00:07:30.910
get out of there as fast as I could.

00:07:32.540 --> 00:07:35.600
And I'm talking, I didn't even get home,

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:38.033
like, when I got out of
there it was July 4th,

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:43.443
2015, I think?

00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:46.243
Maybe 2014.

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:51.340
July 4th, and I didn't even get home yet.

00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:52.810
I had my mom stop off somewhere

00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:53.910
where I knew I could pick up,

00:07:53.910 --> 00:07:56.130
I called my drug dealer from the rehab.

00:07:56.130 --> 00:07:56.963
- [Interviewer] Oh, really?

00:07:56.963 --> 00:07:57.796
- I was like, "Hey",
you know, "What's up?"

00:07:57.796 --> 00:07:59.057
And he said "Where you been at man?

00:07:59.057 --> 00:08:00.157
"Like what happened that day

00:08:00.157 --> 00:08:01.540
"you were supposed to
come and get that sack?"

00:08:01.540 --> 00:08:03.317
And I was like, "You'll never guess, bro,

00:08:03.317 --> 00:08:05.575
"I got in a fucking bad
motorcycle accident,

00:08:05.575 --> 00:08:07.607
"I've been in the hospital, you know,

00:08:07.607 --> 00:08:09.107
"but hey, I don't got cash,

00:08:09.107 --> 00:08:10.427
"I have my EBT card on me.

00:08:10.427 --> 00:08:12.522
"Let's work something out."

00:08:12.522 --> 00:08:13.710
Like, you know, before I even got home,

00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:16.040
I had my mom stop off somewhere

00:08:16.040 --> 00:08:17.050
that I knew I could meet him.

00:08:17.050 --> 00:08:20.503
And I got a sack already,
and I was back on it already.

00:08:21.670 --> 00:08:25.180
So I mean I'm downing Percocets,
you know, back at home.

00:08:25.180 --> 00:08:28.630
They were giving me Ambien
to go to sleep and shit,

00:08:28.630 --> 00:08:30.280
and I was taking them like candy.

00:08:31.291 --> 00:08:32.887
And that kinda like went
on for a little bit,

00:08:32.887 --> 00:08:36.260
And I kind of was using the the accident

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:39.127
to maybe kinda squeeze some money

00:08:39.127 --> 00:08:42.000
out of my mom at the time, you know?

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:44.260
Cause I was telling her that, you know,

00:08:44.260 --> 00:08:46.900
I ran out of my prescription, you know,

00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:50.550
and we tried getting another, basically,

00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:52.913
going to I think a clinic,

00:08:54.497 --> 00:08:55.330
or I forgot how we were

00:08:55.330 --> 00:08:57.350
trying to get another prescription for me.

00:08:57.350 --> 00:08:58.650
But, you know, basically,

00:08:58.650 --> 00:09:01.680
cause I had ran through
the original ones too fast,

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:04.150
you know, I couldn't fill the new one.

00:09:04.150 --> 00:09:07.150
You know, so I wasn't
able to get it filled,

00:09:07.150 --> 00:09:09.570
and I was like, "Oh, how
are we going to do this?"

00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:10.627
So I started telling my mom like,

00:09:10.627 --> 00:09:12.497
"Oh, well I know somebody
who's got these pills.

00:09:12.497 --> 00:09:15.460
"Like, you know, I can go
pick up some off of them."

00:09:15.460 --> 00:09:17.870
So that became like the
new daily, like, you know,

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:18.953
I wasn't even having
to trade pills anymore,

00:09:18.953 --> 00:09:21.203
I was just getting money
to do this and that.

00:09:22.855 --> 00:09:25.020
And basically just
waiting for my settlement

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:28.104
to come through, you know,
cause of the accident,

00:09:28.104 --> 00:09:30.720
you know, it wasn't my fault at all.

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:32.590
A car didn't see me,

00:09:32.590 --> 00:09:34.484
and they turned out right in front of me,

00:09:34.484 --> 00:09:36.883
and I belt up right into it, you know,

00:09:36.883 --> 00:09:38.833
and I was going about 80 miles an hour.

00:09:40.171 --> 00:09:42.112
So, yeah, getting pills.

00:09:42.112 --> 00:09:43.677
I was telling my mom I
was getting the pills,

00:09:43.677 --> 00:09:46.240
but I was using it for heroin, obviously.

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:47.080
You know, because I mean,

00:09:47.080 --> 00:09:48.590
I don't know if you know too much

00:09:48.590 --> 00:09:51.840
about like chemical dependency,
and like, you know, heroin,

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:55.660
but once you get to that
point of an opiate, you know,

00:09:55.660 --> 00:09:57.760
lesser ones don't work, obviously.

00:09:57.760 --> 00:09:59.710
You know, so I can sit there all day long

00:09:59.710 --> 00:10:02.820
and take as many like pills as I want,

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:06.440
but because of the heroin,
it's not going to do too much.

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:08.820
You know, so obviously I was
spending the money on heroin,

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:10.740
and basically at that point just waiting

00:10:10.740 --> 00:10:13.443
for my cash settlement to
come through, you know.

00:10:15.340 --> 00:10:18.610
I had a lawsuit going with basically

00:10:19.489 --> 00:10:21.570
the driver's insurance, you know,

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:25.600
just kind of, you know,
trying to get money

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:27.360
to just take care of myself
for a little bit, you know,

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:29.460
which obviously didn't work out that well.

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:33.460
It went through, you
know, I got my settlement.

00:10:33.460 --> 00:10:35.750
Because I didn't actually
have active insurance

00:10:35.750 --> 00:10:37.530
on my motorcycle at the time,

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:38.960
it kind of fucked me pretty bad,

00:10:38.960 --> 00:10:41.000
and I was only able to
get like a hundred grand

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:42.310
out of the accident.

00:10:42.310 --> 00:10:45.500
Which, 30 of it, 30,000 went
to my attorney at the time.

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:46.333
- [Interviewer] Oh, yeah.

00:10:46.333 --> 00:10:48.960
- And because I was on Medi-Cal,

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:51.420
I don't know if you know
too much about Medi-Cal,

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:53.400
and how they do all that bullshit,

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:57.940
but I had to pay back
some of my medical bills,

00:10:57.940 --> 00:11:02.820
which was up to about $24,000
I had to pay for that.

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:04.820
So, I mean, I got

00:11:06.980 --> 00:11:08.310
50 or so, you know,

00:11:08.310 --> 00:11:11.430
50 some, $56,000 from that.

00:11:11.430 --> 00:11:13.100
- [Interviewer] You lost
a hand and got $50,000.

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:14.250
- Yeah, you know.

00:11:15.612 --> 00:11:16.577
And a lot of people were kinda

00:11:16.577 --> 00:11:18.477
"Oh yeah, I'd do that in a heartbeat,"

00:11:19.314 --> 00:11:21.010
but a lot of people don't really realize

00:11:21.010 --> 00:11:22.113
like what comes with that, you know.

00:11:22.113 --> 00:11:24.590
Like I was right-handed when
this happened, you know,

00:11:24.590 --> 00:11:26.723
obviously, you know I'm left-handed now.

00:11:27.820 --> 00:11:29.260
I used to be a little bit ambidextrous,

00:11:29.260 --> 00:11:31.380
you know, when I was
back in my stoner days

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:34.000
I thought like it was funny
to do shit left-handed,

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:35.650
so I mean, I write better
now with my left hand

00:11:35.650 --> 00:11:36.558
than I ever did with my right.

00:11:36.558 --> 00:11:37.391
- [Interviewer] Oh, interesting.

00:11:37.391 --> 00:11:38.224
- Yeah, so,

00:11:39.940 --> 00:11:40.773
I spent a lot of time

00:11:40.773 --> 00:11:42.873
trying to do that though, too, you know.

00:11:45.541 --> 00:11:46.901
- [Interviewer] What have you been doing

00:11:46.901 --> 00:11:48.308
since the bike accident?

00:11:48.308 --> 00:11:51.980
- (lips blowing) Shit, I bought a house,

00:11:51.980 --> 00:11:53.000
or I started renting,

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:54.890
or I started mortgaging
a house, basically.

00:11:54.890 --> 00:11:57.370
My mom moved out with her boyfriend,

00:11:57.370 --> 00:11:59.580
so I took over the lease on that house.

00:11:59.580 --> 00:12:01.330
And slowly but surely that house

00:12:01.330 --> 00:12:04.090
became a trap house in
Lancaster, pretty much.

00:12:04.090 --> 00:12:05.460
(laughing) I mean, there was so many drugs

00:12:05.460 --> 00:12:07.400
going in and out of it,
and my friends were just

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:09.070
like staying here and there.

00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:10.697
Girlfriend at the time was living with me,

00:12:10.697 --> 00:12:13.190
and just, it went fast, you know.

00:12:13.190 --> 00:12:15.320
All the drugs, and you know,

00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:19.000
I was getting probably
$7,000 every two weeks

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:21.687
on checks from my attornies.

00:12:23.190 --> 00:12:25.750
And I mean, I'm talking by
the two weeks was coming up,

00:12:25.750 --> 00:12:27.271
I was already waiting for that
next check to come through.

00:12:27.271 --> 00:12:29.554
(laughing)

00:12:29.554 --> 00:12:31.110
And I mean, a lot of
people don't really realize

00:12:31.110 --> 00:12:32.640
how fast you can spend money.

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:33.990
- Yeah.
- But it goes man.

00:12:35.710 --> 00:12:36.720
But ever since then,

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:38.820
like as soon as I kind of lost that house,

00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:43.040
that's when, I guess, a stint of like

00:12:44.517 --> 00:12:46.120
kind of my second or third
round of being homeless

00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:48.260
kind of started, you know.

00:12:48.260 --> 00:12:49.990
When I was living in in the Valley,

00:12:49.990 --> 00:12:51.240
you know, selling heroin,

00:12:53.030 --> 00:12:54.330
my money, me and my friends money

00:12:54.330 --> 00:12:56.480
kind of ran out, and the
Airbnbs kind of stopped.

00:12:56.480 --> 00:12:58.780
So basically we started living in the car,

00:12:58.780 --> 00:13:01.180
or I started living with
my girlfriend, in her car,

00:13:01.180 --> 00:13:02.500
You know, we were renting U-Hauls,

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:06.088
kind of sleeping in those
for a bit, you know.

00:13:06.088 --> 00:13:07.077
So I was already kind of used to,

00:13:07.077 --> 00:13:08.840
you know, sleeping outside.

00:13:08.840 --> 00:13:10.180
The drugs helped, you know,

00:13:10.180 --> 00:13:12.210
it wasn't like I was dealing
with too much outside.

00:13:12.210 --> 00:13:13.887
It was like, okay, I'm
nodding off here or there,

00:13:13.887 --> 00:13:15.820
it wasn't that bad.

00:13:15.820 --> 00:13:19.020
But Lancaster, the weather
is just so extreme, bro.

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:19.880
- [Interviewer] Yeah, it's cold at night.

00:13:19.880 --> 00:13:21.480
- Yeah, cold as hell at night.

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:24.580
And this time it was just
about to be winter time,

00:13:24.580 --> 00:13:26.564
or winter time was just ending,

00:13:26.564 --> 00:13:28.514
so I was freezing my ass off out there.

00:13:30.246 --> 00:13:32.190
And as soon as basically summertime

00:13:32.190 --> 00:13:33.413
started to roll around,

00:13:35.720 --> 00:13:37.520
me and the girlfriend were trying

00:13:39.132 --> 00:13:41.040
to basically get sober, in a sense.

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:43.490
You know, trying do it
together, that whole deal.

00:13:45.250 --> 00:13:48.370
So I came out to my sister's
house, down over here,

00:13:48.370 --> 00:13:52.120
or at my sister's apartment,
off the Expo Line, actually.

00:13:52.120 --> 00:13:55.070
She used to live off the
Los Cienega, in Jefferson.

00:13:55.070 --> 00:13:56.450
It was a pretty nice area, you know,

00:13:56.450 --> 00:13:59.180
and our plan was like,
you know, get sober,

00:13:59.180 --> 00:14:01.070
and then we'll figure it
out, go to rehab, you know,

00:14:01.070 --> 00:14:03.560
do that or, you know,
figure something else out.

00:14:03.560 --> 00:14:05.310
So we're out here for about a week,

00:14:06.881 --> 00:14:09.040
and you know, medically, you know,

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:11.230
we did it with like Suboxone
and Subutex, like, you know,

00:14:11.230 --> 00:14:14.020
so it was pretty clean, it wasn't too bad,

00:14:14.020 --> 00:14:16.263
you know, except for the
mental part of everything.

00:14:18.280 --> 00:14:20.660
And just about when that
week started ending up,

00:14:20.660 --> 00:14:22.110
you know, she got a little restless,

00:14:22.110 --> 00:14:24.760
and basically made up an
excuse to kind of turn up

00:14:24.760 --> 00:14:26.590
back out in Lancaster.

00:14:26.590 --> 00:14:28.370
And being the idiot I am, you know,

00:14:28.370 --> 00:14:30.497
I followed her, and went back out there,

00:14:30.497 --> 00:14:33.453
and started using again, you know.

00:14:34.830 --> 00:14:37.550
So, after that I got sucked back out

00:14:37.550 --> 00:14:40.300
in Lancaster, and was doing
the whole homeless thing,

00:14:40.300 --> 00:14:44.043
kind of like stopping
by my moms, you know,

00:14:44.043 --> 00:14:44.937
like "Can I sleep in your car?

00:14:44.937 --> 00:14:46.770
"It's cold out here,
let me charge my phone",

00:14:46.770 --> 00:14:48.830
like all that bullshit, you know.

00:14:48.830 --> 00:14:50.170
Just realizing how like slowly

00:14:50.170 --> 00:14:52.913
I'm fucking killing my mom
with all this bullshit.

00:14:54.592 --> 00:14:57.260
And I never tried to
ask to stay at her place

00:14:57.260 --> 00:14:58.093
with her boyfriend,

00:14:58.093 --> 00:15:00.200
because her boyfriend was
an ex-addict, you know,

00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:01.960
he used to do, I think, crack,

00:15:01.960 --> 00:15:04.760
and you know, basically
was drinking a lot.

00:15:04.760 --> 00:15:07.280
So I wasn't trying to bring my problems

00:15:07.280 --> 00:15:08.190
over to his house, you know?

00:15:08.190 --> 00:15:11.520
Cause I felt like I might lead
him to relapse or something.

00:15:11.520 --> 00:15:12.865
I know how all that bullshit works.

00:15:12.865 --> 00:15:13.860
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:15:13.860 --> 00:15:16.223
So I never asked, I was like,
you know, "I'll stop by,

00:15:17.101 --> 00:15:18.217
"like I'm not gonna not
going to try and be here,

00:15:18.217 --> 00:15:21.840
"and use, or anything like
that around you guys."

00:15:21.840 --> 00:15:23.760
So I just stayed out, you know,

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:25.440
wandering around, and hanging out,

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:27.240
staying with friends here and there.

00:15:28.860 --> 00:15:30.960
And she was getting pretty tired of it.

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:33.136
So I mean, at that point I was like,

00:15:33.136 --> 00:15:34.977
"All right, well let's try and get sober.

00:15:34.977 --> 00:15:37.100
"Let's go do something to do this."

00:15:37.100 --> 00:15:40.810
So I got myself into acting rehab,

00:15:40.810 --> 00:15:44.283
out there, right before
Palmdale, on like the 14.

00:15:46.330 --> 00:15:48.230
I made it about two days,

00:15:48.230 --> 00:15:51.280
cause they don't actually detox you there,

00:15:51.280 --> 00:15:53.053
they want you to be
clean before you show up.

00:15:53.053 --> 00:15:53.920
(interviewer speaking softly)

00:15:53.920 --> 00:15:56.690
- Yeah, so I went in there with fake piss,

00:15:56.690 --> 00:15:58.237
and some drugs, trying to just,

00:15:58.237 --> 00:16:01.646
"Okay, I'm gonna wean
myself off, and stay here,"

00:16:01.646 --> 00:16:03.350
and work it out that way.

00:16:03.350 --> 00:16:07.113
Well, somebody ended up
saying something, or whatever,

00:16:08.222 --> 00:16:09.890
and you know, I got busted

00:16:09.890 --> 00:16:11.117
after like two or three days being there,

00:16:11.117 --> 00:16:12.377
and got kicked out.

00:16:14.900 --> 00:16:15.850
Which I didn't really care,

00:16:15.850 --> 00:16:18.917
I was like, "Whatever, I'm
going to call my girlfriend.

00:16:18.917 --> 00:16:20.317
"figure something else out."

00:16:21.970 --> 00:16:24.945
After a couple of weeks,
or a couple of months,

00:16:24.945 --> 00:16:28.000
of just like doing the
whole homeless thing again,

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:30.587
were basically my mom
called me and told me that

00:16:30.587 --> 00:16:34.927
"I got you a place to
go, and detox, on Monday,

00:16:34.927 --> 00:16:38.477
"so show up, or not, but that's all you."

00:16:41.412 --> 00:16:44.063
So I partied my ass off
that weekend, you know,

00:16:44.063 --> 00:16:45.720
I had some drugs left over,

00:16:45.720 --> 00:16:49.213
and I went and detoxed
down in Pomona at A-R-C,

00:16:51.070 --> 00:16:54.510
ended up staying there,
detoxed there for about a week,

00:16:54.510 --> 00:16:56.313
and moved to the program downstairs.

00:16:59.815 --> 00:17:02.000
And complete, successfully
did it for about three months,

00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:06.100
you know, I was clean, and I was planning

00:17:06.100 --> 00:17:08.743
with my counselor there,
staying there for four months.

00:17:09.720 --> 00:17:13.010
But just about when the three
month mark started coming up,

00:17:13.010 --> 00:17:14.650
he takes me aside and tells me

00:17:15.713 --> 00:17:17.373
that basically your
application for, you know,

00:17:17.373 --> 00:17:19.910
another month here didn't go through,

00:17:19.910 --> 00:17:24.535
and, you mean, you got two
days left to hit 90 days,

00:17:24.535 --> 00:17:28.110
and you're going to get
kicked out, basically.

00:17:28.110 --> 00:17:31.800
You know, I have no plan at this point,

00:17:31.800 --> 00:17:33.517
you know, I'm just like back at step one,

00:17:33.517 --> 00:17:36.490
"Like oh, fuck, like
what am I going to do?"

00:17:36.490 --> 00:17:39.470
And I panicked, and relapsed, you know,

00:17:39.470 --> 00:17:42.620
and then ended up getting
kicked out of the program.

00:17:42.620 --> 00:17:45.940
Basically, I was tired of
telling my mom that, you know,

00:17:45.940 --> 00:17:47.560
I'm doing drugs, all this and that,

00:17:47.560 --> 00:17:49.700
so I couldn't call her anymore.

00:17:49.700 --> 00:17:51.610
It was breaking my heart just to like

00:17:51.610 --> 00:17:54.210
try and tell her these
stories, like you know,

00:17:54.210 --> 00:17:56.377
asking for money, or anything like that.

00:17:57.249 --> 00:17:58.983
So I was like, kind of
at that point decided,

00:17:58.983 --> 00:18:00.120
well, you know, if I'm going to use,

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:01.370
I might as well just go somewhere

00:18:01.370 --> 00:18:02.770
where it's kind of familiar,

00:18:04.162 --> 00:18:06.530
and I can kind of get away
with just being homeless.

00:18:06.530 --> 00:18:10.000
So I came down to Skid Row, you know.

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:12.070
Luckily, I kind of, the month prior,

00:18:12.070 --> 00:18:14.063
ran into somebody I knew down here.

00:18:15.100 --> 00:18:17.703
I actually, coincidentally,
used to sell him drugs

00:18:17.703 --> 00:18:21.480
out in Lancaster, and I
ran into him down here,

00:18:21.480 --> 00:18:24.230
and so that's the first person I called.

00:18:24.230 --> 00:18:25.357
I told him, like "Hey, bro,

00:18:25.357 --> 00:18:27.557
"I just got kicked out
of my sister's house,

00:18:28.607 --> 00:18:29.807
"I'm going to come down to Skid Row,

00:18:29.807 --> 00:18:31.207
"can you help me set up a tent somewhere?

00:18:31.207 --> 00:18:33.910
"Like, do you know any like
safe spot, like you know."

00:18:33.910 --> 00:18:36.950
Basically came down here one night,

00:18:36.950 --> 00:18:40.400
spent that night in
his friend's apartment,

00:18:40.400 --> 00:18:44.580
and the next morning shipped
off to set up a tent, you know?

00:18:44.580 --> 00:18:45.700
That actually, coincidentally,

00:18:45.700 --> 00:18:47.770
it was just down the road right over here.

00:18:47.770 --> 00:18:49.230
I've actually been down over here

00:18:49.230 --> 00:18:51.743
in this little area for about a year now,

00:18:55.220 --> 00:18:56.650
pretty much just doing it,

00:18:56.650 --> 00:18:59.453
you know, just kind of
hustling up where I can.

00:19:01.870 --> 00:19:02.973
I partially,

00:19:03.830 --> 00:19:05.760
I don't know if you've ran into people

00:19:05.760 --> 00:19:07.620
telling you about
boosting, or anything like.

00:19:07.620 --> 00:19:09.520
- Yeah.
- But I mean, yeah, yeah.

00:19:09.520 --> 00:19:12.593
So, I mean, I don't like to
be referred to it as that,

00:19:12.593 --> 00:19:14.550
I mean, I kind of tell people sometimes

00:19:14.550 --> 00:19:17.308
I'm in product redistribution.

00:19:17.308 --> 00:19:18.990
(interviewer laughing)

00:19:18.990 --> 00:19:21.996
So I mean, you know, it's
quite lucrative, you know,

00:19:21.996 --> 00:19:25.510
some of these prices down here, you know,

00:19:25.510 --> 00:19:26.910
down here in MacArthur Park.

00:19:28.156 --> 00:19:30.110
But, you know, if you know what they want,

00:19:30.110 --> 00:19:33.182
and you can get away with
getting enough of it, you know,

00:19:33.182 --> 00:19:35.332
you can buy the world
these days, you know.

00:19:37.154 --> 00:19:39.473
But pretty much just I've been doing that,

00:19:40.350 --> 00:19:42.113
selling drugs, you know.

00:19:43.010 --> 00:19:46.155
I've had a couple offers
to be selling myself

00:19:46.155 --> 00:19:47.780
but I just can not do that, you know.

00:19:47.780 --> 00:19:50.550
And it's all from men,
too, and I'm not gay.

00:19:50.550 --> 00:19:52.976
So it's just like, "No."

00:19:52.976 --> 00:19:54.610
(interviewer speaking softly)

00:19:54.610 --> 00:19:55.865
- Yeah, you know, yeah.

00:19:55.865 --> 00:19:58.060
(interviewer speaking softly)

00:19:58.060 --> 00:19:59.900
You would you be surprised, sometimes,

00:19:59.900 --> 00:20:01.150
I'm not gonna lie to you.

00:20:03.021 --> 00:20:04.188
But that just.

00:20:06.870 --> 00:20:07.703
Honestly,

00:20:08.870 --> 00:20:10.700
a lot of people tell
stories about how bad it is

00:20:10.700 --> 00:20:11.960
down here, and this and that,

00:20:11.960 --> 00:20:14.440
but you know, I'm one of those people

00:20:14.440 --> 00:20:16.350
that kind of just like,

00:20:16.350 --> 00:20:18.323
I look on the brighter
sides of everything.

00:20:19.286 --> 00:20:22.151
And like, honestly,
it's been bad out here,

00:20:22.151 --> 00:20:23.160
like I've been pistol-whipped,

00:20:23.160 --> 00:20:26.010
I've been fucking gashed
on my head, like you know,

00:20:26.010 --> 00:20:26.910
and that's like the worst thing

00:20:26.910 --> 00:20:27.950
that's ever happened to me down here.

00:20:27.950 --> 00:20:30.030
And that's all due to something I did,

00:20:30.030 --> 00:20:32.100
there was nothing on just like accident,

00:20:32.100 --> 00:20:33.820
like that's just
something I did, you know,

00:20:33.820 --> 00:20:35.773
being a dumb drug addict, you know.

00:20:36.720 --> 00:20:37.743
And like, you know,

00:20:39.570 --> 00:20:41.260
I faced it, and I had to handle it.

00:20:41.260 --> 00:20:43.920
But you know, it's not that bad,

00:20:43.920 --> 00:20:45.650
as long as you can kind
of surround yourself

00:20:45.650 --> 00:20:48.300
with people who, you know,
at the time I guess care,

00:20:48.300 --> 00:20:49.910
but just seem to care about you, you know?

00:20:49.910 --> 00:20:51.040
Cause when it comes down to it,

00:20:51.040 --> 00:20:53.380
all of these people do
care about themselves.

00:20:53.380 --> 00:20:54.620
And if you can kind of learn
to just take care of yourself,

00:20:54.620 --> 00:20:56.770
it's not too bad.

00:20:56.770 --> 00:20:59.517
You know, I've kind of gotten my niche,

00:21:00.953 --> 00:21:02.370
and kinda gotten just points

00:21:02.370 --> 00:21:04.110
where I can take care of myself,

00:21:04.110 --> 00:21:06.933
or surround with people that
actually like, you know,

00:21:09.320 --> 00:21:11.393
take care of me, and I take care of them.

00:21:12.767 --> 00:21:15.200
So you know, it hasn't
been too rough of a year.

00:21:15.200 --> 00:21:17.497
But, you know, this is only
my first year, you know,

00:21:17.497 --> 00:21:20.120
and I haven't had to do it too long.

00:21:20.120 --> 00:21:22.943
But, you know, it is
